    # 51..65: Step-by-step reasoning / problem solving (15)
    step_data = [
        ("Separation scheme for sand, salt, and water", "Describe the complete step-by-step laboratory procedure to separate a mixture of sand and salt and recover both components dry and pure.",
         "Student tries to pick sand grains out of dry salt with tweezers.",
         "1. Add water to the sand-salt mixture and stir until salt dissolves. 2. Filter the mixture through filter paper in a funnel: insoluble sand remains as residue; clear salt solution passes through as filtrate. 3. Wash and dry the sand residue. 4. Heat the filtrate in an evaporating dish until all water evaporates, recovering dry salt crystals.",
         "This classic multi-stage separation scheme pairs selective dissolution with filtration and thermal evaporation to cleanly isolate both solid components.",
         "Step 1: Dissolution: pour the dry sand and salt mixture into a beaker containing 100 mL of water; stir with a glass rod until all salt crystals dissolve completely.\nStep 2: Funnel preparation: fold a circular filter paper into quarters, open into a cone, and seat it inside a glass funnel; moisten with water.\nStep 3: Filtration: pour the mixture into the funnel; insoluble sand grains cannot pass through the pores and collect on the paper (Residue).\nStep 4: Residue recovery: wash the sand on the filter paper with a little distilled water, remove, and leave in a warm place to dry -> Pure dry sand recovered.\nStep 5: Evaporation: pour the clear salt solution (Filtrate) into a porcelain evaporating basin; place over a Bunsen burner or water bath.\nStep 6: Crystallization: heat until all liquid water boils off as steam; white sodium chloride crystals coat the basin -> Pure dry salt recovered.\nStep 7: Verify both components are recovered with zero loss.",
         "Heating before filtering, or throwing away the filtrate thinking it was just plain dirty water.",
         "Disassembling a mixture by dissolving the soluble part, straining the solid, and drying the liquid.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What substance is collected as the residue on the filter paper in Step 3?",
         "Drill the complete multi-stage separation pipeline: Dissolve -> Filter -> Evaporate."),

        ("Filter paper cone preparation protocol", "Describe the step-by-step laboratory method to fold and fit a circular filter paper disc into a glass funnel for filtration.",
         "Student crumples the filter paper into a messy ball and shoves it down the funnel neck.",
         "1. Fold circular filter paper in half to make a semicircle. 2. Fold in half again to make a quarter circle. 3. Open one outer flap to form a hollow cone with three layers on one side and one layer on the other. 4. Seat inside glass funnel. 5. Moisten with a few drops of water so it clings snugly to glass walls.",
         "Proper filter paper folding creates a smooth, leakproof conical barrier. Wetting the paper with water eliminates air gaps between paper and glass, ensuring liquid filters through the paper rather than bypassing down the sides.",
         "Step 1: Take a flat, clean circular filter paper disc.\nStep 2: Fold the disc exactly in half along the diameter to create a semicircle.\nStep 3: Fold in half a second time to form a quarter circle (a 90-degree sector); crease edges firmly.\nStep 4: Separate the layers at the top: open one paper layer on one side and three paper layers on the opposite side to form a hollow symmetrical cone.\nStep 5: Insert the cone into a clean 60-degree glass funnel.\nStep 6: Wet the paper with 3-4 drops of distilled water from a wash bottle and press gently with fingertips against the glass walls to seal air pockets.\nStep 7: Verify funnel is ready: liquid will now filter cleanly through the cellulose cone without leaks.",
         "Tearing the paper cone, or opening two layers on each side (which creates a flat wedge with no opening).",
         "Folding a paper party hat so it forms a hollow cone that fits snugly on your head.",
         "Physical activity", "Practice folding a circular filter paper into a cone: verify that one side has 3 layers and one side has 1 layer.",
         "Teach standard laboratory physical manipulation technique for analytical filtration."),

        ("Calculating salt recovery percentage from brine", "A student dissolves 25 grams of salt in 100 mL of water. After boiling to dryness in an evaporating dish, the recovered salt weighs 23.5 grams. Calculate the percentage recovery and account for the missing 1.5 grams.",
         "Student says 1.5 grams of salt evaporated into the air as salt gas.",
         "Recovery is 94.0%. (Calculation: [23.5 g ÷ 25.0 g] × 100 = 94.0%). The missing 1.5 grams was lost due to tiny salt crystals splattering out of the dish during the final stages of boiling or sticking to the stirring rod.",
         "Salt has an extremely high boiling point (1,465°C) and cannot evaporate at 100°C. Mass deficits in evaporation experiments are physical transfer losses (splattering and adhesion), not gaseous vaporization.",
         "Step 1: Identify initial mass of dissolved salt: M_initial = 25.0 grams.\nStep 2: Identify recovered mass of dry salt: M_recovered = 23.5 grams.\nStep 3: Calculate percentage recovery: Recovery % = (M_recovered ÷ M_initial) × 100 = (23.5 ÷ 25.0) × 100 = 94.0%.\nStep 4: Calculate mass deficit: Loss = 25.0 g - 23.5 g = 1.5 grams (6.0% loss).\nStep 5: Physical explanation of loss: during vigorous boiling near dryness, steam bubbles burst and splatter microscopic salt crystals out of the basin; small amounts adhere to the glass stirring rod.\nStep 6: Conclude salt did not evaporate; mass was lost mechanically.",
         "Believing table salt evaporates into air alongside water vapour at 100°C.",
         "Baking cookies: a little dough sticks to the bowl and spoon, so the cookies weigh slightly less than the bowl ingredients.",
         "None", "At what temperature does common table salt boil into a gas: 100°C or over 1,400°C?",
         "Teach quantitative yield calculation and physical error analysis in evaporation experiments."),

        ("Separating immiscible liquids with separating funnel", "Write the step-by-step protocol to separate 50 mL of mustard oil from 100 mL of water using a glass separating funnel in a science lab.",
         "Student shakes the funnel with the bottom tap open.",
         "1. Close bottom stopcock. 2. Pour oil-water mixture into funnel and insert stopper. 3. Mount vertically on retort stand and allow to rest for 10 minutes until two distinct layers form. 4. Remove top stopper. 5. Open stopcock to drain lower water layer into Beaker A. 6. Close stopcock when oil boundary reaches tap. 7. Drain upper oil layer into Beaker B.",
         "Separating funnels exploit immiscibility and density differences to cleanly withdraw the denser bottom liquid while retaining the lighter floating liquid.",
         "Step 1: Verify bottom stopcock is turned perpendicular (closed); clamp funnel vertically on an iron stand.\nStep 2: Pour the mixed oil and water through the top neck using a small glass funnel; insert glass stopper.\nStep 3: Allow the funnel to stand undisturbed for 10 minutes: water (density 1.0 g/cm³) settles at bottom; mustard oil (density 0.91 g/cm³) floats on top.\nStep 4: Remove the top glass stopper (crucial step: equalizes atmospheric pressure so liquid flows smoothly).\nStep 5: Place Beaker A beneath the stem; slowly open the stopcock to allow water to drain in a steady stream.\nStep 6: Watch the descending oil-water boundary closely: when the interface touches the stopcock bore, close the tap immediately.\nStep 7: Place Beaker B beneath stem; open stopcock to collect pure mustard oil.",
         "Forgetting to remove the top stopper, causing liquid to stop draining due to internal partial vacuum.",
         "Opening the drain plug in a bathtub to let water out while toy boats float on top.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a separating funnel showing the exact position of the stopcock when closed versus open.",
         "Drill precision operational procedure for separating funnels in school chemistry."),

        ("Preparing a saturated solution and determining solubility", "Design a step-by-step procedure to determine the maximum solubility of common salt in 100 mL of water at 25°C.",
         "Student adds 1 kg of salt all at once into a tiny test tube.",
         "1. Measure exactly 100 mL of water in a beaker at 25°C. 2. Weigh 50 g of salt on a balance. 3. Add salt 2 grams at a time, stirring thoroughly until each portion dissolves. 4. Stop when salt crystals no longer dissolve and remain at the bottom. 5. Weigh the remaining unused salt. 6. Dissolved mass = Initial mass - Remaining mass.",
         "Solubility determination requires controlled incremental solute addition to a fixed solvent volume at a monitored temperature until the saturation threshold is reached.",
         "Step 1: Measure 100.0 mL of distilled water using a graduated cylinder; pour into a clean 250 mL beaker.\nStep 2: Check water temperature with a thermometer; verify it is at room temperature (25.0°C).\nStep 3: Weigh out exactly 50.0 grams of dry fine common salt on an electronic balance (M_start).\nStep 4: Using a small spatula, add salt in small 2-gram increments, stirring continuously with a glass rod until fully dissolved before adding the next portion.\nStep 5: When approximately 36 grams have been added, notice that salt crystals begin to linger at the bottom despite 2 minutes of vigorous stirring (saturation reached).\nStep 6: Weigh the remaining unused salt on the balance (M_remain = 14.0 grams).\nStep 7: Calculate solubility: Dissolved salt = 50.0 g - 14.0 g = 36.0 grams per 100 mL water at 25°C.",
         "Adding salt in massive chunks that make detecting the exact saturation point impossible.",
         "Adding sugar cubes one by one to a cup of coffee until the last cube stops dissolving.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What is the approximate solubility of common salt (NaCl) in 100 mL of water at 25°C?",
         "Teach quantitative solubility determination protocols using mass balance subtraction."),

        ("Constructing a multi-stage field water filter", "Describe step-by-step how a camper can construct a working emergency water filter using an inverted plastic bottle, cloth, charcoal, sand, and pebbles.",
         "Student pours muddy water through an empty plastic bottle with a hole in the bottom.",
         "1. Cut the bottom off a plastic bottle and invert it. 2. Plug the neck with clean cotton cloth. 3. Add a layer of crushed activated charcoal (absorbs chemicals/odors). 4. Add a layer of fine sand (traps small particles). 5. Add a layer of coarse sand and pebbles on top (traps leaves/twigs). 6. Pour muddy water in top; collect clear water at bottom. 7. Boil clear water before drinking.",
         "A multi-layer column filter uses graded particle sieving from coarse to fine, paired with activated charcoal adsorption, to systematically clarify turbid river water in wilderness survival.",
         "Step 1: Take a clean 2-litre plastic soda bottle; cut off the base to create a wide funnel opening; invert so the cap neck points downward.\nStep 2: Remove cap; pack a dense wad of clean cotton cloth or gauze into the narrow neck to prevent filter materials from falling out.\nStep 3: Add Layer 1 (Bottom): 3 cm of crushed wood charcoal (adsorbs organic impurities, bad smells, and dissolved toxins).\nStep 4: Add Layer 2: 5 cm of clean, washed fine sand (micro-filtration layer trapping fine silt).\nStep 5: Add Layer 3: 5 cm of coarse sand (traps medium sand grains).\nStep 6: Add Layer 4 (Top): 5 cm of smooth small pebbles and gravel (macro-filtration layer trapping floating twigs and leaves).\nStep 7: Pour muddy pond water gently onto the top pebble layer; clear filtered water drips slowly from the neck into a clean jar below.\nStep 8: Critical safety step: boil the collected clear water vigorously for 10 minutes before drinking.",
         "Putting the fine sand on top of pebbles (so the fine sand washes down and clogs the filter immediately).",
         "A multi-layer sieve tower where big rocks stop at the top, sand stops in the middle, and clean water drips out the bottom.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a cross-section of the 4-layer bottle filter labelling Pebbles, Coarse Sand, Fine Sand, and Charcoal.",
         "Teach DIY survival filtration engineering and graded depth filtration."),

        ("Separating camphor and salt by sublimation", "Camphor changes directly from solid to gas when heated (sublimation). Describe the step-by-step laboratory method to separate a mixture of camphor powder and common salt.",
         "Student tries to dissolve camphor in water with salt.",
         "1. Place camphor-salt mixture in a porcelain china dish on a tripod stand. 2. Invert a glass funnel over the dish. 3. Plug the funnel stem with cotton wool. 4. Heat dish gently with a burner: camphor sublimes into white vapour, rises, and condenses as pure solid camphor crystals on the cool inner funnel walls; non-volatile salt remains behind in the dish.",
         "Sublimation separates a volatile sublimable solid (camphor, ammonium chloride, naphthalene) from a non-sublimable solid (salt, sand) through phase change directly from solid to gas and back to solid.",
         "Step 1: Mix 2 g of powdered camphor and 2 g of common salt thoroughly in a porcelain evaporating basin.\nStep 2: Place the basin on a wire gauze mounted on a tripod stand.\nStep 3: Invert a clean, dry glass funnel over the basin so its wide rim covers the mixture.\nStep 4: Plug the open narrow stem of the funnel with a small piece of cotton wool (prevents camphor vapour from escaping into the room).\nStep 5: Heat the basin gently with a small Bunsen burner flame.\nStep 6: Observation: camphor transitions directly from solid to white vapour (sublimation); upon touching the cooler glass walls of the inverted funnel, vapour deposits directly back into pure solid white crystals (deposition/sublimation).\nStep 7: Once white fumes cease, turn off the burner; salt remains in the dish; scrape pure camphor crystals from the funnel walls.",
         "Forgetting to plug the funnel stem with cotton, allowing all camphor vapour to escape into the classroom.",
         "Dry ice turning directly into smoky gas without melting into liquid puddle.",
         "Diagram", "Label the inverted funnel, cotton plug, sublimed camphor crystals, and china dish on a sublimation apparatus diagram.",
         "Teach sublimation as a specialized physical separation technique."),

        ("Separating chalk from iron filings and salt", "You are given a dry 3-component mixture: Iron filings, Chalk powder, and Common salt. Outline a 4-step separation plan to isolate all three substances in pure dry form.",
         "Student uses a sieve to separate chalk from salt.",
         "1. Pass a magnet through the dry mixture to pull out magnetic Iron filings. 2. Add water to remaining chalk and salt: salt dissolves, chalk remains insoluble. 3. Filter through filter paper: insoluble chalk collects as residue on paper; dry it. 4. Boil the salt solution filtrate to dryness to recover salt crystals.",
         "A structured separation flowchart targets one physical property differential per step: magnetism first, solubility second, filtration third, and thermal evaporation fourth.",
         "Step 1: Magnetic extraction: spread the dry mixture on a clean sheet of paper; pass a bar magnet wrapped in plastic wrap closely over the mixture.\n  - Iron filings are ferromagnetic and cling to the magnet -> Transfer to Container 1 (Pure Iron Filings recovered).\nStep 2: Selective dissolution: transfer the remaining chalk and salt powder into a beaker containing 100 mL of water; stir with a glass rod.\n  - Salt dissolves completely into clear solution; chalk powder remains as an insoluble white suspension.\nStep 3: Filtration: pour the mixture through a funnel lined with filter paper into a flask.\n  - Chalk particles cannot pass through paper pores; collect on filter paper as residue. Rinse with a little distilled water and air dry -> Container 2 (Pure Chalk recovered).\nStep 4: Evaporative crystallization: pour the clear filtrate into an evaporating basin and boil over a burner until all water evaporates.\n  - Dry white sodium chloride crystals coat the basin -> Container 3 (Pure Salt recovered).\nStep 5: Confirm all three original dry components are recovered with zero cross-contamination.",
         "Adding water before using the magnet, making wet iron filings rust and clump into chalk.",
         "Sorting coins with a magnet first, then washing the rest in water to separate soluble candy from insoluble plastic tokens.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Which separation tool must be used while the mixture is completely dry: the magnet or the water beaker?",
         "Enforce rigorous procedural sequencing in multi-component separation design."),

        ("Purifying muddy water using alum flocculation protocol", "Write the step-by-step procedure to demonstrate how alum accelerates the clarification of muddy water in a 500 mL glass beaker.",
         "Student throws whole giant rocks of alum into the mud and walks away.",
         "1. Fill two 500 mL beakers with identical muddy river water (Beakers A and B). 2. Leave Beaker A untreated as a control. 3. Tie a small piece of alum to a thread, swirl it 10-15 times in Beaker B, and remove. 4. Observe both beakers every 5 minutes for 30 minutes. 5. Beaker B forms heavy flocs and clears completely in 15 minutes, while Beaker A remains cloudy.",
         "Controlled scientific experimentation requires an untreated control beaker to prove that chemical coagulation (alum) accelerates sedimentation far beyond natural gravity settling alone.",
         "Step 1: Prepare two identical 500 mL transparent glass beakers; label Beaker A ('Control') and Beaker B ('Alum Treated').\nStep 2: Add 400 mL of uniformly stirred turbid muddy river water to both beakers.\nStep 3: Leave Beaker A untouched on the bench.\nStep 4: Take a clean piece of alum (phitkari, ~5 grams) tied to a cotton thread; immerse in Beaker B and swirl gently in circular motions exactly 15 times, then withdraw the alum.\nStep 5: Start a timer and observe both beakers without bumping the bench.\nStep 6: At 5 minutes: Beaker B develops visible snowflake-like fluffy clumps (flocs); Beaker A shows zero change.\nStep 7: At 15 minutes: flocs in Beaker B have settled to the bottom as a compact sediment layer, leaving the upper water sparkling clear.\nStep 8: Contrast with Control A: Beaker A remains muddy and brown throughout, proving alum accelerated sedimentation.",
         "Failing to maintain an untreated control beaker, making it impossible to prove alum caused the rapid settling.",
         "Racing two identical toy cars, but giving one car a rocket booster to prove the booster made it faster.",
         "Physical activity", "Perform the two-beaker alum sedimentation test: photograph both beakers at 0 min, 10 min, and 20 min.",
         "Drill the scientific method: experimental variable vs controlled benchmark."),

        ("Measuring evaporation rate variation with surface area", "Design a step-by-step experiment to test whether water evaporates faster from a wide shallow plate than from a narrow tall test tube when both contain exactly 20 mL of water.",
         "Student uses different amounts of water in each container.",
         "1. Measure exactly 20 mL of water into a wide flat petri dish (large surface area) and 20 mL into a narrow tall graduated cylinder (small surface area). 2. Place both side-by-side in the same room. 3. Measure remaining water volume every 2 hours for 6 hours. 4. Water evaporates significantly faster from the wide dish because evaporation is a surface phenomenon.",
         "Evaporation occurs exclusively at the liquid-air interface. A wider surface area exposes vastly more water molecules to air and heat simultaneously, multiplying the rate of escape.",
         "Step 1: Standardize variables: identical room temperature, identical humidity, zero direct breeze.\nStep 2: Use a graduated pipette to measure exactly 20.0 mL of water into Container 1 (Wide shallow dish, surface diameter = 15 cm).\nStep 3: Measure exactly 20.0 mL of water into Container 2 (Narrow graduated cylinder, surface diameter = 2 cm).\nStep 4: Place both containers side-by-side on a laboratory table.\nStep 5: After 4 hours, pour remaining water from the wide dish into a measuring cylinder to read volume; read volume in the tall cylinder.\nStep 6: Typical observation: wide dish has only 12 mL left (8 mL evaporated); tall cylinder has 19.5 mL left (only 0.5 mL evaporated).\nStep 7: Conclude evaporation rate is directly proportional to exposed surface area.",
         "Failing to standardize initial water volume or placing containers in different environments (e.g. one in sun, one in shade).",
         "Spreading a wet shirt wide on a clothesline makes it dry in an hour; leaving the wet shirt rolled up in a tight ball takes two days to dry.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Why do people spread out wet clothes on a clothesline rather than leaving them folded in a pile?",
         "Connect surface area physics to everyday laundry drying habits."),

        ("Separating immiscible liquid drops from soup (Fat skimming)", "Describe a 3-step physical cooling method used in home kitchens to separate solidified fat from hot meat or chicken soup.",
         "Student pours the boiling soup through paper napkins, burning their fingers.",
         "1. Cool hot soup to room temperature, then place in refrigerator for 3 hours. 2. Low temperature solidifies the floating animal fat into a firm, hard white crust on top of the liquid broth. 3. Lift the solid fat crust off the top with a spoon, leaving low-fat clear liquid soup below.",
         "Fats have a higher freezing/melting point (30-40°C) than water (0°C). Chilling the soup solidifies the lighter floating fat into a rigid plate while water remains liquid, allowing effortless mechanical skimming without filtering.",
         "Step 1: Cool hot soup down and place the covered pot in a refrigerator at 4°C for 2 to 4 hours.\nStep 2: Phase transformation: dissolved/floating animal lipids undergo liquid-to-solid phase transition at refrigerator temperatures, forming a congealed solid white disc.\nStep 3: Density factor: fat is less dense than water (0.92 g/cm³), so it solidifies exclusively at the top liquid surface.\nStep 4: Mechanical removal: slide a flat spoon or spatula under the solid fat disc and lift it out in solid pieces.\nStep 5: Conclude phase-change separation isolates fat cleanly without losing broth.",
         "Trying to skim hot liquid fat with a slotted spoon while it is still liquid and slips through.",
         "Lifting an ice lid off a bucket of cold water.",
         "Physical activity", "Look at cold chicken soup from the fridge: observe the white solid fat layer resting on top of the jelly-like broth.",
         "Teach phase-change separation in domestic food preparation."),

        ("Recovering pure water from saltwater: Simple distillation", "Describe the step-by-step laboratory method to recover BOTH pure drinking water AND dry salt from a sample of seawater using a distillation apparatus.",
         "Student boils water in an open dish and watches all water vanish into the sky.",
         "1. Place seawater in a round-bottom distillation flask connected to a Liebig water condenser and collection beaker. 2. Heat flask with a burner: water boils at 100°C into steam. 3. Steam passes into the inner tube of the condenser, where cold running water cools it back into liquid water (condensation). 4. Pure distilled water drips into collection beaker; solid salt remains in the flask.",
         "Simple distillation combines evaporation and condensation in a single closed system. Boiling vaporizes the volatile solvent, leaving non-volatile solute behind; cooling condenses the vapor, capturing 100% of both components.",
         "Step 1: Pour 150 mL of seawater into a round-bottom distillation flask; add a few boiling chips to ensure smooth boiling.\nStep 2: Insert a rubber stopper with a thermometer in the flask neck; align thermometer bulb with side delivery arm.\nStep 3: Connect delivery arm to a Liebig water condenser; connect rubber tubing so cold tap water enters the outer jacket at bottom and exits at top.\nStep 4: Place a clean Erlenmeyer flask beneath the condenser adapter spout to collect distillate.\nStep 5: Heat distillation flask with a Bunsen burner: at 100°C, water boils vigorously into steam.\nStep 6: Non-volatile salt cannot vaporize and stays in the flask; steam travels into condenser, cools against cold glass walls, and condenses into pure liquid water.\nStep 7: Clear pure water (distillate) collects in the receiver; dry white salt remains in the boiling flask.",
         "Connecting cooling water tubes backwards, or failing to seal joints, letting steam escape.",
         "Catching steam from a boiling kettle on a cold pan lid and letting the clean drops trickle into a glass.",
         "Diagram", "Label distillation flask, thermometer, Liebig condenser, cooling water inlet/outlet, and receiver flask on a distillation diagram.",
         "Teach closed-system distillation for simultaneous solvent and solute recovery."),

        ("Testing saturated solution cooling: Crystal growth", "A student dissolves copper sulphate in hot water (70°C) until no more dissolves (saturated), then filters the hot solution and allows it to cool slowly undisturbed for 24 hours. Describe step-by-step what happens.",
         "Student thinks the blue liquid will freeze into blue ice cubes.",
         "As the hot saturated solution cools, water can hold less dissolved solute; excess copper sulphate molecules are forced out of solution, slowly locking together into large, beautiful deep-blue diamond-shaped (triclinic) copper sulphate crystals.",
         "Solubility decreases with cooling. Slow, undisturbed cooling allows solute molecules to arrange into regular crystalline lattices, growing large geometric seed crystals (crystallization).",
         "1. Hot saturation: at 70°C, 100 mL water holds ~75 g copper sulphate.\n2. Filtration: filtering while hot removes dust particles that would trigger premature chaotic crystallization.\n3. Cooling phase: as temperature drops to 25°C, solubility drops from 75 g to 32 g per 100 mL.\n4. Supersaturation: the extra 43 grams cannot remain dissolved.\n5. Nucleation and growth: solute ions attach layer-by-layer onto crystal faces, forming large, sharp, rhombic deep-blue copper sulphate crystals on the bottom and sides.\n6. Conclude crystallization purifies and recovers solid substances in crystalline form.",
         "Shaking or bumping the cooling beaker, causing microscopic ugly sludge instead of large clean crystals.",
         "Snowflakes forming in cold clouds: water molecules slowly locking into perfect hexagonal geometric stars.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What geometric shape do slow-grown copper sulphate crystals exhibit under a magnifying glass?",
         "Teach slow crystallization and crystal lattice growth from cooling saturated solutions."),

        ("Separating husk from grain using winnowing machine (Blower)", "How does a modern agricultural winnowing machine separate wheat grains from broken chaff without waiting for outdoor wind?",
         "Student thinks the machine uses giant magnets to attract wheat.",
         "Harvested threshed mixture is fed into a top hopper that drops it across a controlled horizontal blast of air from an electric fan blower: heavy wheat grains fall straight down into an exit chute, while light chaff is blown out the side discharge duct.",
         "Agricultural winnowing machines mechanize the traditional wind method: an electric centrifugal fan creates constant, adjustable airflow that separates components continuously regardless of outdoor weather.",
         "1. Input: mixture of wheat grain, chaff, and light dust enters via a vibrating feed hopper.\n2. Regulated dropping: a vibrating gate drops a thin, uniform curtain of mixture across an air channel.\n3. Artificial wind: an electric blower fan blows a steady stream of air horizontally across the falling curtain.\n4. Differential deflection: dense wheat grains have high inertia and drop into the central grain chute.\n5. Chaff ejection: light chaff flakes are carried horizontally by the air blast out through a separate exhaust duct.\n6. Efficiency: processes 500 to 2,000 kg of grain per hour continuously in indoor barns.",
         "Assuming agricultural machinery must rely on complex chemicals rather than basic airflow physics.",
         "A hair dryer blowing dry leaves off a driveway while heavy stones stay firmly in place.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a schematic of an electric winnowing machine showing hopper, blower fan, grain chute, and chaff exhaust.",
         "Show how traditional physics principles are scaled into modern agricultural machinery."),

        ("Testing water clarity across progressive filtration stages", "A student collects 1 litre of dirty river water and processes it through three stages: Stage 1 (Decantation after 10 min), Stage 2 (Sand filtration), Stage 3 (Double-layer filter paper). Describe the step-by-step visual and turbidity changes.",
         "Student thinks Stage 1 decantation makes water 100% clean.",
         "Raw water: dark brown, muddy, full of floating twigs and leaves. Stage 1 (Decantation): leaves heavy sand behind, but water remains yellowish-brown and cloudy with suspended silt. Stage 2 (Sand filter): removes coarse silt, yielding faintly hazy pale yellow water. Stage 3 (Filter paper): removes all suspended particles, yielding sparkling crystal-clear transparent water.",
         "Progressive multi-stage filtration systematically reduces turbidity by targeting smaller particle size thresholds at each successive stage.",
         "Step 1: Raw river water inspection: dark brown, opaque, rapid sedimentation of heavy pebbles, floating organic matter, turbidity >100 NTU.\nStep 2: Stage 1 (Decantation): pouring off top liquid leaves gravel and sand behind; liquid remains turbid and cloudy (turbidity ~50 NTU) with suspended clay.\nStep 3: Stage 2 (Sand-gravel column filter): water passes through sand pores; traps medium silt; water emerges translucent with faint yellow tint (turbidity ~10 NTU).\nStep 4: Stage 3 (Whatman filter paper): micro-pores (<10 microns) trap all microscopic suspended clay particles; water emerges sparkling clear and transparent (turbidity <1 NTU).\nStep 5: Conclude each stage progressively eliminates finer particle fractions.",
         "Assuming a single separation step achieves maximum clarity.",
         "Rough sanding with coarse sandpaper, then medium sandpaper, then ultra-fine sandpaper to make wood glassy smooth.",
         "Virtual Lab", "In which stage (1, 2, or 3) did the water first become completely transparent without cloudiness?",
         "Establish the quantitative progression of multi-stage clarification.")
    ]

