         "Petroleum hydrocarbons have different molecular chain lengths: small molecules have low boiling points and rise to the cool top before condensing; large heavy molecules have high boiling points and condense near the hot bottom.",
         "1. Input: crude petroleum heated in a furnace to ~380-400°C into vapour and liquid sludge.\n2. Column physics: 50-metre tall fractionating tower containing horizontal bubble-cap trays; bottom is ~350°C, top is ~20°C.\n3. Condensation by height:\n  - Top (coolest, <40°C): Refinery gas (methane, propane, butane) remains gas.\n  - Upper trays (40-100°C): Petrol/Gasoline (C5-C8 chains) condenses.\n  - Middle trays (150-250°C): Kerosene/Jet fuel (C9-C12) condenses.\n  - Lower trays (250-350°C): Diesel oil (C13-C20) condenses.\n  - Bottom (hot residue, >350°C): Heavy fuel oil, lubricating wax, and Asphalt/Bitumen for roads.\n4. Conclude multi-stage continuous thermal separation powers global transportation energy.",
